{"qid":"Q123027565","names":["Kashmiri Sultanate"],"title":"Kashmiri Sultanate","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kashmir Sultanate (1320–1589) was an independent Islamic state centered on Srinagar that succeeded the Hindu Lohara dynasty after Rinchan Shah, a Ladakhi Buddhist convert to Islam, seized power in 1320. Its apogee came under Sultan Zayn al-ʿĀbidīn (r. 1420–1470), known as Bud Shāh, who patronized Persian letters and Sanskrit scholarship, lifted restrictions on non-Muslims, and maintained diplomatic ties from Central Asia to Delhi. Sikandar Butshikan (r. 1389–1413) had earlier pursued an aggressive policy of temple demolition and forced conversion of Kashmiri Pandits. Mughal emperor Akbar conquered the valley in 1586, and the last Chak ruler Yūsuf Shāh surrendered in 1589.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial extent (the Kashmir Valley) is well attested; the precise outer limits of vassalage — notably at the sultanate's apex under Shihāb al-Dīn in the 1360s — rely on chronicle accounts and remain approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Kashmir Sultanate","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kashmir-Sultanate"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kashmir Sultanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kashmir_Sultanate"},{"label":"Encyclopaedia Iranica: Kashmir","url":"https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/kashmir"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kashmir Sultanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Shah Mir","nativeName":"شاہ میر","roman":"Shāh Mīr","start":1339,"end":1342,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Jamshid Shah","native